# simple.phext — Will's Twitter posts in Up Goer Five

This is a special phext. It holds every post Will Bickford put on
Twitter, from 2008 to 2025 — 29539 posts — each one turned into
Up Goer Five, the form of English that uses only the ten hundred
most used words. (The list itself has some holes: it has one, two,
three, four, five, six, seven, ten, twenty, and hundred, but no
eight, no nine, and no thousand — so numbers in the posts stay as
numbers, and a billion is said the long way: ten hundred ten
hundred ten hundreds.)

Each post sits at the place it belongs in word space. A post's
place is found the holographic way, the Singular way: take the
post's own distinct words, find where each word sits in the
Singular atlas (the average of every Choose Your Own Adventure
scroll that holds it — words in his archive but not in that book
rest at home, 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1, and words in neither rest at the
far corner, 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9), and average the places. Posts near
each other here are near each other in what they say. If two posts
land on the same place, the later one steps one scroll over, so no
post ever covers another.

How the translation was made, in short: web addresses and @-names
were dropped (they are addresses, not speech). A word already in
the ten hundred, or a changed form of one, stays as it was. Other
words were traded for ten-hundred words by a substitution map —
names are told in simple words there ("the talking place" for
Twitter, "the word place I made" for phext) — or cut down to a
listed base form. What no rule could carry is held by a plain type
word — person, place, thing, group, other — never dropped without
a mark. The map itself is kept in this phext, on the shelf below,
so anyone can check the trades.

How to read it: the timeline comes first. One index scroll per
year sits at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.2.1 (for 2008) through 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.2.18
(for 2025); each lists that year's posts in time order with the
coordinate of each post's scroll, so you can walk the years in
order. Or skip the line and wander by place: open any coordinate
and read what gathers there. Every post scroll starts with its
date (dates and times are in UTC, as the archive keeps them),
then the post in simple words.

One honest note: the post scrolls were checked with the Up Goer
Five checker and hold zero words outside the ten hundred. This
Home scroll, the year indexes, and the map headers are the frame —
plain writing, not under that check, and this note says so where
you can see it.

The first words live in Will's Twitter archive; the word places
come from Singular. The map holds 1439 trades in 24
scrolls. This phext holds 29539 post scrolls and 18
year index scrolls, plus this Home and the map.

— built 2026-11-07
